Create a durable queue named order-events .Durable means the queue definition survives a RabbitMQ restart ,although message durability also depends on how message are published

First check wheather the queue already exits or not:

>> kubectl exec -n rabbitmq rabbitmq-0 -- \
     rabbitmqctl list_queue name durable  messages 


Step 1: Create  the order event queue using the HTTP API

We'll forward the management port to your local machine, then use curl to create the queue. This avoids installing anything inside the RabbitMQ container.

>> kubectl port-forward -n rabbitmq svc/rabbitmq 15672:156672

Create the Order Event 

>> RABBITMQ_PASSWORD="$(kubectl get secret rabbitmq-auth -n rabbitmq -o jsonpath='{.data.rabbitmq-password}' | base64 -d)"

curl --fail-with-body --user "ecommerce-worker:${RABBITMQ_PASSWORD}" \
  -H "content-type: application/json" \
  -X PUT \
  -d '{"durable":true,"auto_delete":false,"arguments":{}}' \
  http://127.0.0.1:15672/api/queues/%2F/order-events

unset RABBITMQ_PASSWORD


verify order-events exits or not:

>> RABBITMQ_PASSWORD="$(kubectl get secret rabbitmq-auth -n rabbitmq -o jsonpath='{.data.rabbitmq-password}' | base64 -d)"

curl --silent --show-error --user "ecommerce-worker:${RABBITMQ_PASSWORD}" \
  --output /dev/null \
  --write-out 'HTTP status: %{http_code}\n' \
  http://127.0.0.1:15672/api/queues/%2F/order-events

unset RABBITMQ_PASSWORD 

Verify the messages in the Queue:

>> kubectl exec -n rabbitmq rabbitmq-0 -- \
  rabbitmqctl list_queues name durable messages



